  "properties": {
    "encrypted": "false",
    "long_instructions": "## Filter Logic with ECHO\r\n\r\nWe will use the **ECHO** framework to build our movie filters: **E**xplore, **C**o-Create, **H**ighlight, **O**wn.\r\n\r\n### 1. Explore (Ask AI)\r\nFirst, we need to find only \"Sci-Fi\" movies. Ask the AI:\r\n> *\"Show me two different ways to create a filter that returns only movies where genre === 'Sci-Fi' using .map() and conditionals.\"*\r\n\r\n**Compare:** Which one looks cleaner? \r\n\r\n### 2. Co-Create (Refine Logic)\r\nChoose **one version** and paste it into the `genreBtn` event listener.\r\n1.  **Test:** Does it work?\r\n2.  **Refine:** Ask AI: *\"Can you make this version show both 'Sci-Fi' and 'Comedy' movies?\"*\r\n3.  **Edge Case:** Ask AI: *\"What happens if a movie is missing a genre property? How do I fix that?\"*\r\n4.  **Update:** Update your code with the fixes.\r\n\r\n### 3. Highlight (Best Practices)\r\nLook at your final code in `genreBtn`. Note the strong practices:\r\n* **Start with empty array**\r\n* **Handle missing values**\r\n* **Clean Output**\r\n\r\n### 4. Own (Build Your Own)\r\nNow, move to the `yearBtn` listener. Write this logic **yourself**:\r\n1.  **Goal:** Filter the list to show only movies released **after 2020**.\r\n2.  **Write Code:** Use the pattern you highlighted above (empty array + map + if).\r\n3.  **Validate:** Ask AI: *\"Generate 3 test cases to verify my year filter logic, including one edge case.\"*",
